==Dutch==
{{wikipedia|lang=nl}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|nl|odt|*graf}}, from {{inh|nl|gem-pro|*grabą}}, {{m|gem-pro|*grabō||grave, trench, ditch}}, from {{der|nl|ine-pro|*gʰrābʰ-||to dig, scratch, scrape}}. Compare Low German {{m|nds|Graf}}, {{m|nds|Graff}}, German {{m|de|Grab}}, West Frisian {{m|fy|grêf}}, English {{m|en|grave}}, Danish {{m|da|grav}}, Icelandic {{m|is|gröf}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{rhymes|ɑf|lang=nl}}
* {{audio|Nl-graf.ogg|audio|lang=nl}}

====Noun====
{{nl-noun|n|-:ven|grafje}}

# [[grave]]

=====Derived terms=====
* [[grafsteen]]

====Related terms====
* {{l|nl|graven}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{der|nl|fr|grave||serious, grave}}. Most likely influenced by Dutch {{m|nl|erg||}} which can mean "serious, grave" as well as "very". The alternative form {{m|nl|graaf||very}}, also slang, has the same origin and meaning, but stays closer to the original French pronunciation.

====Pronunciation====
{{rfp|lang=nl|Same as above for all etymologies? If so move the Pron section above the first etymology section at L3.}}

====Adverb====
{{nl-adv}}

# {{label|nl|slang}} [[very]]
#: ''Dat is graf duur'' &mdash; That's very expensive

